WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.265
What's the difference between this shot

00:00:03.265 --> 00:00:04.658
and this shot?

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.024
They're from the same franchise,

00:00:08.024 --> 00:00:09.700
just a few years apart.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.320
What exactly is the camera doing

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.770
that makes these similar shots

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.776
feel so different from each other?

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.820
OK, one more.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.403
What about this shot...

00:00:20.775 --> 00:00:21.988
and this shot?

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.247
Did you catch it?

00:00:24.247 --> 00:00:26.340
"Casino Royale" makes two subtle changes

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.340
to the cinematography that result in it

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.230
looking completely different from all

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.170
of the Bond films that precede it.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.053
To find out what exactly it's doing

00:00:34.053 --> 00:00:36.145
and why it's doing it, we have to go back

00:00:36.145 --> 00:00:38.023
to the beginning of the franchise.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.460
For the overwhelming
majority of Bond films

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.777
before "Casino Royale," the cinematography

00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:48.670
appears to have been an afterthought.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.063
The lighting was flat,

00:00:50.063 --> 00:00:51.620
the composition was boring,

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.590
and the cinematography didn't do anything

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.730
to advance character or story.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.950
While action scenes adequately
showcased the set pieces

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.750
and quality set and production design

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.210
helped mask the cinematic shortcomings,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.440
in general, there was very little artistry

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.380
put into the cinematography.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.260
James Bond is supposed
to be a super-sleek spy,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.410
but so many of these clunky
and dull-looking scenes

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.940
did nothing to further that conceit.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.110
This came to a head
after "Die Another Day,"

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.940
when the Bond movie producers decided

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.500
to reboot the franchise

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.560
and make the next film more
grounded and realistic.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.220
Although a great new actor,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.240
a return to practical effects,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.610
and a stripped-down script

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.240
all helped the new dedication to realism,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.253
the cinematography was arguably

00:01:34.253 --> 00:01:37.260
the biggest component
that needed an update.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.630
Cinematography is responsible for creating

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:40.880
the look of a film,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.330
dictating the lighting, composition,

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.650
and camera movement of every shot.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.570
Studying the film, it becomes clear

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.710
that there are two main
principles that guide

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.950
the distinctive cinematography
of "Casino Royale":

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.740
move the camera and reflect the mood.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.540
So, what exactly does that mean?

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.090
To begin, let's talk
about how "Casino Royale"

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.931
moves its camera differently

00:02:04.931 --> 00:02:06.981
from all other Bond films.

00:02:06.981 --> 00:02:09.300
For example, say the
script calls for a scene

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.060
where Bond is talking to M.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.003
As M paces around the room,

00:02:13.003 --> 00:02:16.050
you could film the scene with
fairly standard coverage,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.700
panning the camera to keep
both her and Bond in frame,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.450
or you could have the
camera track M's movement,

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.520
emphasizing her restless mindset

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.670
and using a tighter frame to help foster

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.251
a connection to her character.

00:02:29.251 --> 00:02:30.570
Here's another example:

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.820
two scenes that show an establishing shot

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.241
of a car arriving at a new location.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.040
Notice how the camera staying
still isn't as engaging.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.200
Viewers' eyes are naturally more attracted

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.600
to increased movement.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.280
It's the reason why still images

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.230
on television or YouTube videos

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.250
often have an added camera zoom or pan.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.250
Compare this first
scene of Bond running...

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.710
with this second scene.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.910
Notice how this is much more exciting

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.723
and more effectively
conveys urgency and speed.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.300
Here are a couple other things that

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.520
intelligent camera movement can do:

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.570
exhibit the chaotic
nature of a new location,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.440
amplify a character's dynamic motion,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.400
convey multiple pieces
of information at once,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.170
and the list goes on.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.180
However, adding movement to a shot

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.100
doesn't always make it better.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.800
Nonstop camera movement
can be distracting,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.500
and there generally needs to be some sort

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.820
of motivation for the camera to move.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.010
For example, this interrogation scene

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.450
from "Die Another Day."

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.490
Take a look at this
strange camera movement

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.940
at the end of the first shot.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.200
There's no reason for the
camera to move to the right.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.997
For all of its great camera movement,

00:03:41.997 --> 00:03:45.300
"Casino Royale" still knows
when not to move the camera.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.490
Like early in the film,

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.660
when Le Chiffre is playing
poker on his yacht.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.750
The camera is completely stationary,

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.360
emphasizing Le Chiffre's full
control of the poker game.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.105
Discussing how camera work
influences the tone of a scene

00:03:57.105 --> 00:03:58.650
takes us to "Casino Royale"'s

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.780
second principle of cinematography,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.550
reflecting the mood.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.750
Every film has a mood or tone.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.040
In "The Godfather," the story's focus on

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.300
its characters' internal
battle between good and evil

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.327
is mirrored in its use of
dark and shadowy lighting.

00:04:13.327 --> 00:04:16.280
"Lawrence of Arabia"'s
epic story is emphasized

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.625
with expansive landscapes and uses

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:21.751
70-millimeter film to
create a massive frame.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.060
The mood of every Bond film is suave.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.170
In each film, Bond goes
around solving mysteries,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.780
picking up women, and narrowly
escaping enemy attacks.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.100
He wears nice suits and has a
very particular drink order.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.530
Bond: Shaken, not stirred.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:41.018
Narrator: But this
sleek demeanor is rarely

00:04:41.018 --> 00:04:43.770
reflected in the drab cinematography.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.700
What the cinematography in
"Casino Royale" does so well

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.350
is that it's just as
stylish as its protagonist

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.950
for the duration of the film,

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.520
emphasizing Bond's sleek nature

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.100
and the covert world that he inhabits.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.170
Here's an example.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:59.250
Let's say there's a
simple line in the script

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.850
that describes Bond grabbing a gun

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.970
from the glove compartment of his car.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.668
You could do it in two
shots using an insert...

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.505
or, if you want to emphasize
the sleek nature of the car,

00:05:11.505 --> 00:05:13.493
you can do it all in one shot.

00:05:15.755 --> 00:05:18.217
Again, let's say there's
another basic scene

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:19.730
that needs to be filmed.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.560
This time, it's Bond
talking to a woman in bed,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.740
arguably where James
Bond is at his Bondiest.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.760
You could shoot the
scene with flat lighting

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.020
and standard composition...

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.850
or you could shoot the scene
with stylized lighting,

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:35.710
interesting camera angles,

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.103
and then end the scene with
a fantastic shot like this.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.400
And there are many different
ways that "Casino Royale"

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.570
uses stylish cinematography
to enhance the film.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.330
It uses lighting to emphasize emotions,

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.460
tilted camera angles to
create a sense of imbalance,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:54.440
and color schemes to
reflect character traits.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.940
Every scene in "Casino
Royale" feels like a scene

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.517
that is distinct to a spy movie.

00:05:59.517 --> 00:06:01.633
"Casino Royale" is so confident

00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:03.933
in the effectiveness of
its sleek cinematography

00:06:03.933 --> 00:06:05.780
to capture the essence of Bond

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.430
that instead of putting the iconic

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:09.620
animated women in the title sequence,

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.230
like every other Bond film,

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.450
it creates a better version
of the shot in camera.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:16.640
And it's shots like this, that combine

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.430
camera movement with a
suave cinematic style,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.660
that create some of the
best sequences in the film.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.450
For example, a scene where
a moving camera conveys

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:27.360
the sense of speed and urgency

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.400
and the stylish shot
composition adds to the trill.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.570
How about a shot that
expertly communicates

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.490
the location and distance
between characters?

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.880
Need to have Bond transition

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.950
from one conversation to another?

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.790
You could do it using several clunky cuts,

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.738
or you could do it with
one smooth camera movement.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.040
After the success of "Casino Royale,"

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.840
2012's "Skyfall" actually
doubled down on cinematography,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.810
hiring legendary
cinematographer Roger Deakins

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.000
to create stunning set
pieces and compositions.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.615
Putting cinematography at
the forefront of the film

00:07:02.615 --> 00:07:05.410
was applauded by critics
and audiences alike

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:06.540
and helped "Skyfall" become

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.037
the highest-grossing Bond film ever.

00:07:09.037 --> 00:07:10.880
"Casino Royale" demonstrates that with

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.731
a little bit of extra effort, care,

00:07:13.731 --> 00:07:15.700
and strong cinematic principles,

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.250
you can completely reinvent

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.370
the cinematic look of a franchise

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:20.976
and proves that it's worth the trouble

00:07:20.976 --> 00:07:23.109
when you can transform this...

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:25.084
into this.

